status":null,"result":"I'm approving PKT-0021. The narrowed packet does what its goal asks.\n\n- **Scope narrowing:** dropping item (2) was one of the options the earlier review offered. It now has its own spawn request, `.theseus/spawn/PKT-0021-valid-saves-under-load-replay-a-browser-captured.md`, which states the cause found here. The Goal section says plainly what was moved.\n- **Item (1), 200×5 hang:** the explanation hangs together. The test script never sent `unLockDocument`, so after the 30 s `lockDoc` timer the server dropped the lock holder. Docs were opened one after another, which is why only the 200-doc run went past 30 s, and the `saveLock` wait had no timeout, so the script hung. A before/after comparison backs this up: 71 `noLockReply` and 142 missing broadcasts before the fix, none after. Results for 1,000 and 2,000 sockets are recorded. The packet says honestly that no ceiling was reached and why it stopped there: the load generator shares the VM's 2 CPUs.\n- **Item (3), the 124 s conversion:** it was reproduced. A deck that converts in about 2 s on an idle server took 68.6 s when run right after a load test, because it waited in the shared converter queue (one conversion at a time). The explanation matches PKT-0018's ordering. The packet says the PKT-0018 logs could not be checked, so that link is inferred, not proven.\n- **Contract and target:** the diff touches only `.theseus` queue, spawn and friction files. No source or perf script changed, and the patched scripts stayed in the executor's scratchpad.\n- **TEP-0004 checks:** the Executor log is filled in, both follow-ups are listed under Spawned packets and exist in `.theseus/spawn/`, and the friction lines are present. This is an investigate packet, so the rule about keeping surprises in tests does not apply.\n- **Budget:** 4 files and 167 lines, within 6 files and 800 lines.\n\nThree small issues, none blocking:\n\n1. Attempt 1's line \"Acceptance: none defined, so nothing to run\" is still in